<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<sdf version="1.9">
  <model name="hirrus">
    <!-- Ground clearance from the landing-gear legs below, reused verbatim
         from tricopter_ap/model.sdf (same gear geometry, same airframe). -->
    <pose>0 0 0.246 0 0 0</pose>
    <link name="base_link">
      <enable_wind>true</enable_wind>
      <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <!-- Mass/inertia/CG from Hirrus.avl's own AVL run (~/Hirrus/Polara.txt,
             a Type-7 stability analysis with a loaded mass file), not a
             guess: Mass=14.330 lb, CoG=(0.1431, 0, -0.02155) ft, Ixx/Iyy/
             Izz/Ixz=(28.79, 12.91, 41.67, -0.02858) lb.ft^2, converted to
             SI below. This SUPERSEDES tricopter_ap's own CG derivation
             (which used the .avl file's Xref/Yref/Zref -- the aerodynamic
             moment reference point AVL defaults to, not the mass file's
             actual CG; Polara.txt shows those are NOT the same point here,
             43.6mm vs 260mm aft of the root LE).

             Position: same mesh-alignment method as tricopter_ap (root-band
             leading edge measured on Hirrus.dae at model-frame x=+0.193,
             z=-0.08, see that file's own base_link_visual comment) minus
             the AVL-frame CoG offset from that same LE point:
               x = 0.193 - 0.043617 = 0.149383
               z = -0.08 + (-0.006568) = -0.086568
             Ixx/Iyy/Izz map straight across (both mesh<->AVL frames agree
             on which physical axis is roll/pitch/yaw, only X and Y flip
             sign via the 180deg yaw below) -- diagonal moments are
             sign-invariant. Ixz picks up one sign flip (X flips, Z
             doesn't): +0.001204, not -0.001204.
             Sanity check: Izz(1.756) = Ixx(1.213)+Iyy(0.544) to 3 decimal
             places, exactly the thin-flat-body identity for a tailless
             flying wing with (almost) no mass out of the wing plane --
             good evidence this mass file is real and self-consistent.

             CAVEAT, not silently resolved: this CG sits ~0.26m ahead of
             tricopter_ap's own neutral-point estimate (x=-0.109, area-
             weighted quarter-chord of the two wing panels) -- a ~84% MAC
             static margin, far outside the normal 5-15% MAC range for a
             tailless design. Either that NP estimate doesn't hold once
             the wing's real cla (below) replaces the old placeholder, or
             this specific AVL run's mass file reflects a deliberately
             very-forward/hand-launch-safe CG rather than a cruise-
             optimized one -- not something to guess past. Fly FBWA
             straight-and-level and trim from here, same as any real
             aircraft's first CG check (same advice tricopter_ap's own CG
             comment gives). -->
        <pose>0.149383 0 -0.086568 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>6.500</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>1.213214</ixx>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0.001204</ixz>
          <iyy>0.544029</iyy>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
          <izz>1.755978</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <!-- Silhouette collision box, reused verbatim from tricopter_ap
           (real Hirrus geometry, not VTOL-specific): full span ~3.28m,
           nose-to-trailing-edge ~1.18m, thin ground-contact-only slab. -->
      <collision name="base_link_collision">
        <pose>-0.06 0 -0.02 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>1.18 3.28 0.06</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <surface>
          <contact>
            <ode>
              <kp>100000</kp>
              <kd>10.0</kd>
              <max_vel>10.0</max_vel>
              <min_depth>0.001</min_depth>
            </ode>
          </contact>
          <friction>
            <ode>
              <mu>0</mu>
              <mu2>0</mu2>
            </ode>
          </friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>
      <!-- Whole-file visual (fuselage+wings+elevons all in one), NOT 5
           per-part <submesh> visuals as this used to be. gz-sim's own
           submesh extraction (Mesh/AssimpLoader, whatever <submesh><name>
           matches against) is broken for this file's wing/elevon
           geometry specifically -- confirmed three separate ways live via
           real screenshots (--headless-rendering + the /gui/screenshot
           service, see git history for the images): the node name
           ("aripa_stg"), the raw COLLADA name="..." attribute ("aripa
           stg"), and the geometry id-with-suffix ("aripa_stg-mesh") all
           left the wings/elevons invisible -- only fuselaj and elice ever
           rendered. Same bug reproduced independently in
           AeroCanyon_Guard's own tricopter_ap model (identical submesh
           setup, same mesh file) -- pre-existing in that project too, not
           something introduced here. Loading the whole file with NO
           <submesh> filter at all rendered the complete aircraft
           correctly (full fuselage + both swept wings + winglets) --
           proof the mesh DATA itself is fine and the fault is
           specifically in gz's submesh-name-matching/extraction path.

           Tradeoff accepted, not solved: the file also contains "elice"
           (a static, non-spinning duplicate of the real pusher prop),
           which this whole-file visual now includes too, underneath the
           separate physics-driven "propeller" link below (that link
           keeps its own working elice-only submesh reference -- single-
           part submesh extraction DOES work, it's specifically the
           multi-part case failing here) -- cosmetic double-prop overlap,
           same tradeoff tricopter_ap's own comment (removed by this
           point) originally worked around by excluding elice via
           per-part submeshes, which is exactly the mechanism that's
           broken. Upgrade path: get the wing/elevon submeshes extracting
           correctly (file a gz-sim issue with this repro) and go back to
           per-part visuals, excluding elice again. Same 180deg-yaw
           mounting as before (points the mesh's own nose at +X). -->
      <visual name="base_link_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 3.14159265</pose>
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>model://hirrus/meshes/Hirrus.dae</uri>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>1.0 1.0 1.0 1.0</ambient>
          <diffuse>1.0 1.0 1.0 1.0</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <!-- Motor nacelle: a plain static visual (no joint -- Hirrus's
         single pusher motor doesn't tilt, unlike tricopter_ap's
         motor_N links), same role as that project's motor_N_visual --
         a housing between the fuselage and the prop disc, separate from
         the disc's own spinning visual on the "propeller" link below.
         Cylinder axis rotated onto body X (the prop's own spin axis);
         sits just forward (fuselage side) of the disc at x=-0.495. -->
      <visual name="motor_visual">
        <pose>-0.44 0 -0.06 0 1.5707963 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder><radius>0.035</radius><length>0.09</length></cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.1 0.1 0.1 1.0</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.1 0.1 0.1 1.0</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <!-- Tricycle landing gear added below (nose_wheel/left_main_wheel/
           right_main_wheel) -- normal wheeled takeoff/landing roll on the
           runway now, catapult launch (the ApplyLinkWrench plugin further
           down) shelved for now per pilot's call, not removed.
           base_link_collision below is still the fuselage silhouette --
           now a backup belly-contact shape, not the primary ground
           contact (the wheels are). -->

      <gravity>1</gravity>
      <velocity_decay />
      <self_collide>0</self_collide>

      <sensor name="imu_sensor" type="imu">
        <gz_frame_id>base_link</gz_frame_id>
        <!-- 180deg roll: Gazebo's IMU reports in the SENSOR frame and
             ArduPilotPlugin doesn't rotate it itself, only pose/velocity
             go through modelXYZToAirplaneXForwardZDown. Reused from
             tricopter_ap (same body-frame convention). -->
        <pose degrees="true">0 0 0 180 0 0</pose>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>250</update_rate>
        <imu>
          <angular_velocity>
            <x><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0</mean><stddev>0.0003394</stddev><dynamic_bias_stddev>3.8785e-05</dynamic_bias_stddev><dynamic_bias_correlation_time>1000</dynamic_bias_correlation_time></noise></x>
            <y><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0</mean><stddev>0.0003394</stddev><dynamic_bias_stddev>3.8785e-05</dynamic_bias_stddev><dynamic_bias_correlation_time>1000</dynamic_bias_correlation_time></noise></y>
            <z><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0</mean><stddev>0.0003394</stddev><dynamic_bias_stddev>3.8785e-05</dynamic_bias_stddev><dynamic_bias_correlation_time>1000</dynamic_bias_correlation_time></noise></z>
          </angular_velocity>
          <linear_acceleration>
            <x><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0</mean><stddev>0.004</stddev><dynamic_bias_stddev>0.006</dynamic_bias_stddev><dynamic_bias_correlation_time>300</dynamic_bias_correlation_time></noise></x>
            <y><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0</mean><stddev>0.004</stddev><dynamic_bias_stddev>0.006</dynamic_bias_stddev><dynamic_bias_correlation_time>300</dynamic_bias_correlation_time></noise></y>
            <z><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0</mean><stddev>0.004</stddev><dynamic_bias_stddev>0.006</dynamic_bias_stddev><dynamic_bias_correlation_time>300</dynamic_bias_correlation_time></noise></z>
          </linear_acceleration>
        </imu>
      </sensor>
      <sensor name="air_pressure_sensor" type="air_pressure">
        <gz_frame_id>base_link</gz_frame_id>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>50</update_rate>
        <air_pressure>
          <pressure>
            <noise type="gaussian"><mean>0</mean><stddev>0.01</stddev></noise>
          </pressure>
        </air_pressure>
      </sensor>
      <sensor name="magnetometer_sensor" type="magnetometer">
        <gz_frame_id>base_link</gz_frame_id>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>100</update_rate>
        <magnetometer>
          <x><noise type="gaussian"><stddev>0.0001</stddev></noise></x>
          <y><noise type="gaussian"><stddev>0.0001</stddev></noise></y>
          <z><noise type="gaussian"><stddev>0.0001</stddev></noise></z>
        </magnetometer>
      </sensor>
      <sensor name="navsat_sensor" type="navsat">
        <gz_frame_id>base_link</gz_frame_id>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>30</update_rate>
      </sensor>
    </link>

    <!-- Elevon anchor links: real flap geometry now, extracted from
         Hirrus249.blend's separated ~CS_LMA/~CS_RMA objects (a cleaner,
         newer source model with elevons/propeller/motor as their own
         objects, unlike Hirrus.dae's single fused mesh where gz's
         submesh extraction is confirmed broken for these specific
         parts). Exported to left_elevon.dae/right_elevon.dae by
         re-baking each mesh's own node transform (scale + a 90deg-about-Z
         axis remap from the blend file's own coordinate convention to
         this model's body frame -- verified empirically: LMA/RMA's
         transformed bounding-box centers land within 7mm of this joint's
         own long-trusted hinge position in X/Y, and within 1mm in Z after
         calibrating out a constant ~77mm origin-datum offset between the
         two source files), then recentering each to its own local origin
         so the visual's own <pose> IS that computed centroid with no
         further rotation needed (the mesh is already body-frame-aligned
         on export). Live-verified via screenshot: elevon shape now sits
         flush on the wing trailing edge, matches the real mesh silhouette
         (previously a red box, still red for visibility, but now the
         right shape/size at the right hinge). base_link's own whole-file
         visual above still bakes in the OLD Hirrus.dae eleron_stg/dr
         geometry too (that extraction problem is about a different
         source file and is unrelated/unfixed) -- a cosmetic double-render
         at rest, same accepted tradeoff as the propeller/elice overlap;
         only THIS visual here actually moves with the joint. -->
    <link name="left_elevon">
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.00000001</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.000001</ixx><ixy>0.0</ixy><ixz>0.0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.000001</iyy><iyz>0.0</iyz><izz>0.000001</izz>
        </inertia>
        <pose>0 0.34 0 0 0 0</pose>
      </inertial>
      <visual name="left_elevon_visual">
        <pose>-0.421438 1.242670 -0.111046 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <mesh><uri>model://hirrus/meshes/left_elevon.dae</uri></mesh>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0</ambient>
          <diffuse>1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
    <link name="right_elevon">
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.00000001</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.000001</ixx><ixy>0.0</ixy><ixz>0.0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.000001</iyy><iyz>0.0</iyz><izz>0.000001</izz>
        </inertia>
        <pose>0 -0.34 0 0 0 0</pose>
      </inertial>
      <visual name="right_elevon_visual">
        <pose>-0.422597 -1.240689 -0.109595 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <mesh><uri>model://hirrus/meshes/right_elevon.dae</uri></mesh>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0</ambient>
          <diffuse>1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
    <joint name="left_elevon_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>left_elevon</child>
      <!-- Hinge line measured off eleron_stg (Hirrus.dae), reused verbatim
           from tricopter_ap -- see that file's own comment for the
           derivation (forward edge x=-0.317, midspan y=1.2425, mid
           z=-0.111). -->
      <pose>-0.317 1.2425 -0.111 0 0 0</pose>
      <axis>
        <xyz>0 1 0</xyz>
        <limit>
          <lower>-0.78</lower>
          <upper>0.78</upper>
        </limit>
        <dynamics>
          <damping>1.000</damping>
        </dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>
    <joint name="right_elevon_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>right_elevon</child>
      <pose>-0.317 -1.2425 -0.111 0 0 0</pose>
      <axis>
        <xyz>0 1 0</xyz>
        <limit>
          <lower>-0.78</lower>
          <upper>0.78</upper>
        </limit>
        <dynamics>
          <damping>1.000</damping>
        </dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>

    <!-- Pusher propeller: real "elice" mesh position measured directly off
         Hirrus.dae (bbox center x=0.4950,y=0.0010,z=-0.0604 before the
         180deg yaw, i.e. x=-0.495,y=0,z=-0.06 after it) -- a separate,
         physics-driven link/joint, NOT the static "elice" submesh (that
         one stays out of base_link's visual list above). <center>true</center>
         on the submesh reference re-centers its own baked-in vertex
         coordinates around this link's local origin, so the link's own
         <pose> alone places it correctly (no double offset). Spins about
         the model's local X (fore-aft) axis -- the raw mesh's disk already
         lies in the Y-Z plane at this station. -->
    <!-- 3deg yaw ("right thrust"-style motor offset, same technique real
         tractor singles use to counter torque/gyroscopic yaw tendency,
         adapted here for a pusher): the propeller link's own pose carries
         the offset, so everything defined in ITS local frame -- the
         joint's spin axis (xyz 1 0 0, read in the child link's frame),
         the visual, and the LiftDrag blades' cp/forward/upward -- cants
         together as one rigid unit. Thrust (~90N) times sin(3deg) gives a
         ~4.7N side force acting well aft of the CG (propeller at
         x=-0.495), producing a yawing moment to counteract the
         propeller's own reaction/gyroscopic yaw tendency.
         Tested live at this sign (+3deg) with propeller_joint's damping
         at its normal 0.002: heading holds rock-steady (357.00 -> 356.93
         over 25s) and full-throttle groundspeed still caps at the same
         6.46 m/s as with no offset at all -- so this is confirmed SAFE
         (doesn't destabilize anything) but UNPROVEN to help; it's not
         what's capping the ground-roll speed (see hirrus.parm's own
         "15 m/s" investigation notes). Kept as standard practice, not a
         verified fix. The opposite sign (-3deg) was also tried and was
         equally inert on speed under stable damping.
         Separately (and NOT part of this offset): reducing this joint's
         damping to try to free up torque budget was tested and made
         things much worse -- the aircraft span through nearly every
         heading continuously instead of holding a straight roll. Whatever
         is going on with the propeller's reaction-torque coupling to the
         airframe, the current damping=0.002 is doing real stabilizing
         work; don't lower it without re-verifying live. -->
    <link name="propeller">
      <pose>-0.495 0 -0.06 0 0 0.0523599</pose>
      <inertial>
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>0.005</mass>
        <inertia>
          <!-- ixx (the ONLY component that matters here -- propeller_joint
               is a 1-DOF revolute about local X, so only the spin-axis
               inertia enters the joint's angular-acceleration equation)
               was 9.75e-07: modeled as a ~4.8cm rod ((1/12)*m*L^2 solved
               backwards), way smaller than this prop's own aerodynamic
               radius (the LiftDrag blades' cp sits at 0.0724m, so the
               real blade tip is further out still). At that inertia, the
               VELOCITY control's 8 N-m torque cap changed the joint's
               velocity by ~8000 rad/s in a SINGLE 1ms physics step --
               the PID could never converge, it just slammed straight
               past the 1800 rad/s target every step. Measured live:
               propeller_joint oscillating between ~560 and ~2860 rad/s
               instead of holding ~1800, tanking the time-averaged thrust
               (proportional to velocity^2) -- the real cause of the
               plane plateauing around 9 m/s at full throttle regardless
               of mode. Recomputed as a ~0.2m (20cm) two-blade rod at the
               same 0.005kg mass, consistent with the 0.0724m cp radius:
               (1/12)*0.005*0.2^2 = 1.667e-05. -->
          <ixx>1.667e-05</ixx>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.000166704</iyy>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
          <izz>0.000167604</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <visual name="visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 3.14159265</pose>
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>model://hirrus/meshes/Hirrus.dae</uri>
            <submesh>
              <name>elice</name>
              <center>true</center>
            </submesh>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.175 0.175 0.175 1.0</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.175 0.175 0.175 1.0</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <gravity>1</gravity>
      <velocity_decay />
      <self_collide>0</self_collide>
    </link>
    <joint name="propeller_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>propeller</child>
      <axis>
        <xyz>1 0 0</xyz>
        <dynamics>
          <damping>0.002</damping>
        </dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>

    <!-- Tricycle landing gear: nose wheel forward of the CG (x=0.149,
         see model.config's own comment), two main wheels aft of it --
         standard tricycle layout, CG ahead of the main axle for ground
         stability. Free-rolling only (no steering DOF on the nose wheel,
         no brakes) -- ArduPilot doesn't drive wheels directly on a plain
         ArduPlane frame, they just roll passively under prop thrust and
         are steered aerodynamically (rudder-less here, elevon differential
         isn't real steering, but that's a flight-characteristics problem,
         not a gear-modeling one). Real (non-zero) rolling friction, unlike
         the earlier frictionless ramp attempt -- this is a normal wheeled
         takeoff roll now, not a slide launch.
         Wheel radius 0.05m, axle at local z=-0.15 -> wheel bottom at
         z=-0.20, well clear of the fuselage mesh's own lowest point
         (-0.1341, see model.sdf's earlier catapult-comment history) so
         the belly itself never touches the ground first. -->
    <link name="nose_wheel">
      <!-- z=-0.16, radius 0.05 -> bottom at -0.21, level with the main
           wheels' own bottom (axle -0.15, radius 0.06 -> also -0.21). -->
      <pose>0.35 0 -0.16 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.02</mass>
        <inertia><ixx>0.00002</ixx><iyy>0.00002</iyy><izz>0.00002</izz></inertia>
      </inertial>
      <collision name="nose_wheel_collision">
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder><radius>0.05</radius><length>0.02</length></cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction>
            <ode><mu>1.0</mu><mu2>1.0</mu2></ode>
          </friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>
      <visual name="nose_wheel_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder><radius>0.05</radius><length>0.02</length></cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.1 0.1 0.1 1.0</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.1 0.1 0.1 1.0</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
    <joint name="nose_wheel_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>nose_wheel</child>
      <axis>
        <xyz>0 1 0</xyz>
        <dynamics><damping>0.01</damping></dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>

    <link name="left_main_wheel">
      <pose>0.0 0.3 -0.15 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.03</mass>
        <inertia><ixx>0.00003</ixx><iyy>0.00003</iyy><izz>0.00003</izz></inertia>
      </inertial>
      <collision name="left_main_wheel_collision">
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder><radius>0.06</radius><length>0.025</length></cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction>
            <ode><mu>1.0</mu><mu2>1.0</mu2></ode>
          </friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>
      <visual name="left_main_wheel_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder><radius>0.06</radius><length>0.025</length></cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.1 0.1 0.1 1.0</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.1 0.1 0.1 1.0</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
    <joint name="left_main_wheel_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>left_main_wheel</child>
      <axis>
        <xyz>0 1 0</xyz>
        <dynamics><damping>0.01</damping></dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>

    <link name="right_main_wheel">
      <pose>0.0 -0.3 -0.15 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.03</mass>
        <inertia><ixx>0.00003</ixx><iyy>0.00003</iyy><izz>0.00003</izz></inertia>
      </inertial>
      <collision name="right_main_wheel_collision">
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder><radius>0.06</radius><length>0.025</length></cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction>
            <ode><mu>1.0</mu><mu2>1.0</mu2></ode>
          </friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>
      <visual name="right_main_wheel_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder><radius>0.06</radius><length>0.025</length></cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.1 0.1 0.1 1.0</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.1 0.1 0.1 1.0</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
    <joint name="right_main_wheel_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>right_main_wheel</child>
      <axis>
        <xyz>0 1 0</xyz>
        <dynamics><damping>0.01</damping></dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>

    <!-- Wing/elevon LiftDrag panels: same 4-panel washout split (inner
         fixed / outer elevon per side) and same cp/area geometry as
         tricopter_ap (measured directly off the Hirrus.dae mesh -- see
         that file's own comment for the span-band slicing). cla/cda
         REDERIVED here from Hirrus.avl's own real CL-alpha/drag polar
         (~/Hirrus/Polara.txt, 17-point trimmed sweep from an actual AVL
         solve), replacing tricopter_ap's placeholder cla=5.2 (an
         unconstrained Helmbold-corrected guess) and cda=0.2275 (an
         unchanged X8 leftover):
           cla = 2.48 /rad, a0 = -0.0371 rad -- linear least-squares fit of
           CL vs alpha over the whole 17-point sweep (residual < 0.001).
           cda = 0.0739 /rad -- least-squares fit of Cd = cda*(alpha-a0),
           matching gz-sim-lift-drag-system's own linear (not parabolic)
           drag model; ponytail: that linear-in-alpha model is a poor fit
           to the real (visibly parabolic, induced-drag-dominated) polar
           at the sweep's low-alpha end (~30% under at alpha=0) and closer
           near alpha=8deg -- picked as the best single slope across the
           full sweep since cruise alpha isn't pinned down yet either.
           Upgrade path: refit cda over just the actual trimmed-cruise
           alpha band once one is confirmed by flight-testing this model.
         a0 keeps tricopter_ap's own per-panel split (-0.052 inner /
         -0.021 outer, from Hirrus.avl's SECTION incidence table) rather
         than collapsing to the single whole-aircraft -0.0371: their
         area-weighted average (-0.040) already lands within 0.15deg of
         the fitted value, and the split is what gives the outer/elevon
         panel its self-trimming washout -- collapsing it would remove
         that.
         alpha_stall/cla_stall/cda_stall: UNCHANGED placeholders --
         Polara.txt's sweep only covers alpha<=8.4deg (a normal-cruise
         trim sweep, not a stall test), so no real stall data exists yet
         for this airframe. -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-lift-drag-system" name="gz::sim::systems::LiftDrag">
      <a0>-0.052</a0>
      <cla>2.48</cla>
      <cda>0.0739</cda>
      <cma>0.0</cma>
      <alpha_stall>0.3391428111</alpha_stall>
      <cla_stall>-3.85</cla_stall>
      <cda_stall>-0.9233984055</cda_stall>
      <cma_stall>0</cma_stall>
      <cp>-0.029 0.534 -0.09</cp>
      <area>0.259</area>
      <air_density>1.2041</air_density>
      <forward>1 0 0</forward>
      <upward>0 0 1</upward>
      <link_name>base_link</link_name>
      <control_joint_name>left_elevon_joint</control_joint_name>
      <control_joint_rad_to_cl>-0.26</control_joint_rad_to_cl>
    </plugin>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-lift-drag-system" name="gz::sim::systems::LiftDrag">
      <a0>-0.021</a0>
      <cla>2.48</cla>
      <cda>0.0739</cda>
      <cma>0.0</cma>
      <alpha_stall>0.3391428111</alpha_stall>
      <cla_stall>-3.85</cla_stall>
      <cda_stall>-0.9233984055</cda_stall>
      <cma_stall>0</cma_stall>
      <cp>-0.239 1.207 -0.105</cp>
      <area>0.159</area>
      <air_density>1.2041</air_density>
      <forward>1 0 0</forward>
      <upward>0 0 1</upward>
      <link_name>base_link</link_name>
      <control_joint_name>left_elevon_joint</control_joint_name>
      <control_joint_rad_to_cl>-0.26</control_joint_rad_to_cl>
    </plugin>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-lift-drag-system" name="gz::sim::systems::LiftDrag">
      <a0>-0.052</a0>
      <cla>2.48</cla>
      <cda>0.0739</cda>
      <cma>0.0</cma>
      <alpha_stall>0.3391428111</alpha_stall>
      <cla_stall>-3.85</cla_stall>
      <cda_stall>-0.9233984055</cda_stall>
      <cma_stall>0</cma_stall>
      <cp>-0.029 -0.534 -0.09</cp>
      <area>0.259</area>
      <air_density>1.2041</air_density>
      <forward>1 0 0</forward>
      <upward>0 0 1</upward>
      <link_name>base_link</link_name>
      <control_joint_name>right_elevon_joint</control_joint_name>
      <control_joint_rad_to_cl>-0.26</control_joint_rad_to_cl>
    </plugin>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-lift-drag-system" name="gz::sim::systems::LiftDrag">
      <a0>-0.021</a0>
      <cla>2.48</cla>
      <cda>0.0739</cda>
      <cma>0.0</cma>
      <alpha_stall>0.3391428111</alpha_stall>
      <cla_stall>-3.85</cla_stall>
      <cda_stall>-0.9233984055</cda_stall>
      <cma_stall>0</cma_stall>
      <cp>-0.239 -1.207 -0.105</cp>
      <area>0.159</area>
      <air_density>1.2041</air_density>
      <forward>1 0 0</forward>
      <upward>0 0 1</upward>
      <link_name>base_link</link_name>
      <control_joint_name>right_elevon_joint</control_joint_name>
      <control_joint_rad_to_cl>-0.26</control_joint_rad_to_cl>
    </plugin>
    <!-- Wingtip winglets -- passive, fixed (no control_joint_name), reused
         verbatim from tricopter_ap: no dedicated small-fin airfoil data
         for Hirrus either, same reasoned stand-in. -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-lift-drag-system" name="gz::sim::systems::LiftDrag">
      <a0>0.0</a0>
      <cla>4.752798721</cla>
      <cda>0.6417112299</cda>
      <cma>0.0</cma>
      <alpha_stall>0.3391428111</alpha_stall>
      <cla_stall>-3.85</cla_stall>
      <cda_stall>-0.9233984055</cda_stall>
      <cma_stall>0</cma_stall>
      <cp>-0.30 1.56 -0.03</cp>
      <area>0.025</area>
      <air_density>1.2041</air_density>
      <forward>1 0 0</forward>
      <upward>0 1 0</upward>
      <link_name>base_link</link_name>
    </plugin>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-lift-drag-system" name="gz::sim::systems::LiftDrag">
      <a0>0.0</a0>
      <cla>4.752798721</cla>
      <cda>0.6417112299</cda>
      <cma>0.0</cma>
      <alpha_stall>0.3391428111</alpha_stall>
      <cla_stall>-3.85</cla_stall>
      <cda_stall>-0.9233984055</cda_stall>
      <cma_stall>0</cma_stall>
      <cp>-0.30 -1.56 -0.03</cp>
      <area>0.025</area>
      <air_density>1.2041</air_density>
      <forward>1 0 0</forward>
      <upward>0 1 0</upward>
      <link_name>base_link</link_name>
    </plugin>

    <!-- Propeller thrust rig: ArduPilotPlugin's VELOCITY control only
         spins propeller_joint (zero lift on its own); these two LiftDrag
         blocks at opposite cp (so off-axis moments cancel) convert that
         spin into forward thrust. Coefficients reused verbatim from
         tricopter_ap's own rotor thrust rigs (already sized for a
         same-order-of-magnitude prop on this airframe); cp/forward/upward
         permuted for this prop's spin axis (X here vs Z there). -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-lift-drag-system" name="gz::sim::systems::LiftDrag">
      <a0>0.3</a0>
      <alpha_stall>1.4</alpha_stall>
      <cla>4.2500</cla>
      <cda>0.10</cda>
      <cma>0.0</cma>
      <cla_stall>-0.025</cla_stall>
      <cda_stall>0.0</cda_stall>
      <cma_stall>0.0</cma_stall>
      <area>0.004</area>
      <air_density>1.2041</air_density>
      <cp>0 0.0724 0</cp>
      <forward>0 0 1</forward>
      <upward>1 0 0</upward>
      <link_name>propeller</link_name>
    </plugin>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-lift-drag-system" name="gz::sim::systems::LiftDrag">
      <a0>0.3</a0>
      <alpha_stall>1.4</alpha_stall>
      <cla>4.2500</cla>
      <cda>0.10</cda>
      <cma>0.0</cma>
      <cla_stall>-0.025</cla_stall>
      <cda_stall>0.0</cda_stall>
      <cma_stall>0.0</cma_stall>
      <area>0.004</area>
      <air_density>1.2041</air_density>
      <cp>0 -0.0724 0</cp>
      <forward>0 0 -1</forward>
      <upward>1 0 0</upward>
      <link_name>propeller</link_name>
    </plugin>

    <!-- Required for the elevon servos to work at all: ArduPilotPlugin's
         POSITION+useForce=1 path reads the joint's JointPosition
         component but never creates one itself. propeller_joint doesn't
         need this -- its VELOCITY control reads JointVelocity, already
         populated by physics. -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-joint-state-publisher-system" name="gz::sim::systems::JointStatePublisher">
      <joint_name>left_elevon_joint</joint_name>
      <joint_name>right_elevon_joint</joint_name>
      <!-- propeller_joint doesn't NEED this (see comment above -- its
           VELOCITY control already gets JointVelocity from physics), but
           listing it here also makes gz publish its velocity on this same
           topic, which is otherwise the only way to read prop RPM live
           for debugging (SERVO_OUTPUT_RAW only shows the commanded PWM,
           not the actual achieved spin). Wheel joints listed for the same
           reason -- confirming free rolling (wheel omega matching
           groundspeed/radius) vs. slipping/dragging. -->
      <joint_name>propeller_joint</joint_name>
      <joint_name>nose_wheel_joint</joint_name>
      <joint_name>left_main_wheel_joint</joint_name>
      <joint_name>right_main_wheel_joint</joint_name>
    </plugin>

    <!-- Catapult launch (a manually-fired ApplyLinkWrench impulse) shelved
         per pilot's call -- real tricycle gear (above) does normal
         commanded-throttle wheeled takeoffs now. gz-sim-apply-link-
         wrench-system is no longer declared in any world flying this
         model, so there's no listener for that impulse even if someone
         tried to fire it -- removed on purpose, not an oversight, to
         close off any uncommanded-thrust path. -->

    <plugin name="ArduPilotPlugin" filename="ArduPilotPlugin">
      <!-- 0.0.0.0, not 127.0.0.1: this is the socket bind address for
           incoming servo packets. 127.0.0.1 only accepts them on
           loopback, which drops a SITL/Mission Planner running on the
           Windows host (its packets arrive on the WSL VM's eth
           interface). 0.0.0.0 accepts on any interface; the plugin still
           replies to whatever address the packet came from. Matches
           tricopter_ap. -->
      <fdm_addr>0.0.0.0</fdm_addr>
      <fdm_port_in>9002</fdm_port_in>
      <connectionTimeoutMaxCount>5</connectionTimeoutMaxCount>
      <lock_step>1</lock_step>
      <!-- Explicit, like zephyr_with_ardupilot: without it ArduPilot logs
           "Forcing use_time_sync=0" and silently switches AHRS_EKF_TYPE to
           10 mid-boot. Pin it so the FDM handshake is deterministic. -->
      <no_time_sync>1</no_time_sync>

      <!-- Same body-frame convention as tricopter_ap (identical mesh
           mounting, 180deg yaw). -->
      <gazeboXYZToNED degrees="true">0 0 0 180 0 90</gazeboXYZToNED>
      <modelXYZToAirplaneXForwardZDown degrees="true">0 0 0 180 0 0</modelXYZToAirplaneXForwardZDown>

      <imuName>base_link::imu_sensor</imuName>

      <!-- SERVO1/2 (channels 0/1): elevons, POSITION+useForce, gains
           reused verbatim from tricopter_ap (already tuned there for
           this exact elevon geometry/mass class). multiplier=1.56 maps
           incoming [0,1] normalized PWM (offset=-0.5 -> [-0.5,0.5]) onto
           the +-0.78 rad joint limit. servo_min/max = 1100/1900, matching
           ArduPlane's real SERVO1/2_MIN/MAX defaults (and RC1/2_MIN/MAX,
           see hirrus.parm) -- with the old 1000/2000 here, ArduPilot's own
           output stage clips SERVO1/2 PWM to 1100-1900 before it ever
           reaches gz, so raw only ever spanned [0.1, 0.9] instead of
           [0, 1] and full stick deflection landed at +-0.624 rad, not the
           full +-0.78 -- exactly the "manual stick doesn't reach the
           extremes" symptom. -->
      <control channel="0">
        <jointName>left_elevon_joint</jointName>
        <useForce>1</useForce>
        <multiplier>1.56</multiplier>
        <offset>-0.5</offset>
        <servo_min>1100</servo_min>
        <servo_max>1900</servo_max>
        <type>POSITION</type>
        <p_gain>100.0</p_gain>
        <i_gain>20</i_gain>
        <d_gain>1.0</d_gain>
        <i_max>5</i_max>
        <i_min>-5</i_min>
        <cmd_max>25.0</cmd_max>
        <cmd_min>-25.0</cmd_min>
      </control>
      <control channel="1">
        <jointName>right_elevon_joint</jointName>
        <useForce>1</useForce>
        <multiplier>1.56</multiplier>
        <offset>-0.5</offset>
        <servo_min>1100</servo_min>
        <servo_max>1900</servo_max>
        <type>POSITION</type>
        <p_gain>100.0</p_gain>
        <i_gain>20</i_gain>
        <d_gain>1.0</d_gain>
        <i_max>5</i_max>
        <i_min>-5</i_min>
        <cmd_max>25.0</cmd_max>
        <cmd_min>-25.0</cmd_min>
      </control>
      <!-- SERVO3 (channel 2): throttle, VELOCITY on propeller_joint.
           servo_min/max + cmd_min follow zephyr_with_ardupilot's throttle
           block, NOT the tricopter's rotor blocks: ArduPlane's SERVO3_MIN
           (throttle) defaults to 1100, and disarmed / zero-throttle output
           sits at that PWM. With servo_min=1000 that mapped to raw=0.1 ->
           180 rad/s of commanded prop spin at zero throttle, enough idle
           thrust to creep the airframe down the runway on its own. With
           servo_min=1100 (and cmd_min=0, no reverse thrust) zero throttle
           -> raw=0 -> cmd=0. multiplier/cmd_max still first-pass: the prop
           LiftDrag coeffs are the tricopter rotor's, not a measured prop,
           so max prop RPM and the velocity-PID torque authority still need
           tuning against a real takeoff roll. -->
      <control channel="2">
        <jointName>propeller_joint</jointName>
        <useForce>1</useForce>
        <!-- EFFORT instead of VELOCITY: open-loop constant torque
             (cmd = multiplier*raw directly, no PID) instead of a velocity
             target. Fixes a real bug: the VELOCITY controller was
             limit-cycling between ~1660-1890 rad/s (even after the
             propeller inertia fix elsewhere in this file) instead of
             holding a steady RPM, capping ground-roll speed at ~6.47 m/s
             regardless of throttle. EFFORT is self-limiting instead --
             total resisting torque = damping*omega + k*omega^2
             (damping=0.002 on propeller_joint's own axis, k=5.48e-08
             measured from the blade reaction torque) grows with omega, so
             a constant applied torque settles at whatever omega balances
             it, with no feedback loop to hunt in the first place. Below
             ~5000 rad/s the damping term (linear in omega) dominates the
             aero term (quadratic) by orders of magnitude, so omega is
             essentially just multiplier/damping.
             multiplier=6.0 (omega settles ~2745-2750 rad/s, live-verified
             stable, zero oscillation) took ground-roll speed from the old
             6.47 m/s cap to 12+ m/s -- confirmed via joint_state + VFR_HUD
             together. Groundspeed then peaked ~12.15 m/s and fell back
             (12.15 -> 9.58 -> 6.78) while propeller velocity stayed rock
             steady the whole time -- almost certainly a real liftoff
             (12 m/s is enough dynamic pressure to generate real lift on
             this wing) followed by an uncontrolled pitch excursion, since
             this was tested in MANUAL with the pitch stick held dead
             center, not a real pilot/autopilot managing rotation. That's
             a separate flight-dynamics question now, not a propulsion
             one -- thrust is no longer the bottleneck. Earlier failed
             attempt used multiplier=0.178 (solving only the aerodynamic
             term, forgetting damping) -- prop coasted to a dead stop
             instead of holding any RPM, exactly what that arithmetic
             mistake predicts once you redo it including damping. -->
        <multiplier>6.0</multiplier>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <servo_min>1100</servo_min>
        <servo_max>1900</servo_max>
        <type>EFFORT</type>
      </control>
    </plugin>
  </model>
</sdf>
